# family: scene
# bridge: B(record-trace)
# regime: exact (scene identities, event keys and request ids are symbols and integers)
# instrument-first: d1bb6941
# baseline: tests/unit/scene, tests/integration/scene
# baseline-count: 102
# successor-form: matrix
#
# The unit is the record plane inside SceneCore, which is now NetwSceneCore:
# the observer routing table keyed by scene identity and event, and the one
# scene request in flight.
#
# THE SAME TWO-PLANE SPLIT the three batches before it took, in a fourth place:
#
#   record     the routing table, the id counter, the pending id. Keyed by the
#              scene's entity RID rather than by a container, holds no Node,
#              instantiates nothing.
#   interface  every Node, PackedScene, NetwSceneHandle and NetwPromise verb;
#              the admissions, which name a NetwInterestLayer and therefore
#              cannot precede W4; the deadline timer; the frames.
#
# THIS LEDGER WAS WRITTEN ALL CARRY, and writing it was the point. The class
# crossed and the specification did not, because NOT ONE of the 102 cases is
# pointed at the record plane alone: every case that touches it also drives a
# promise, a node or the api's own surface, which is the half that stays. The
# liveness batch reached the same shape for the same reason and it is a
# legitimate crossing, not a stalled one. What the record plane owes instead is
# stated natively, in nine cases of its own.
#
# THE DECLARED SORT re-read all 102 against a declared world rather than against
# the class, and moved four. It found what the paragraph above predicted: the
# admission edge is the ONLY half of this family a world can declare without a
# tree, so `declared_scene_laws.cpp` states membership, nesting and admission,
# and the four rows it takes are the four that assert an admission and nothing
# else. Every remaining row drives a handle, a participant, a promise, a
# PackedScene or a container shape, and each is a reason rather than a pending
# port. `test_scene_membership_is_ancestry.gd` in `interest.ledger` gave up one
# more row for the same reason, and kept its two visibility probes.
#
# THREE VERBS THE DECLARED SORT FOUND UNREACHABLE, each stated where a law would
# otherwise have read it. `scene_declare` needs a record only binding a node
# creates and binding a node arms it, so the composed sequence its own doc
# states answers ERR_DOES_NOT_EXIST and then ERR_UNCONFIGURED; the declared
# world writes the facet pre-arm as `Netw.configure_multiplayer_scene` does.
# `scene_move` resolves its destination through the registry `tree_entered`
# fills, so a declared container is never a destination and the seat is the
# record's own reparent. `scene_get_layer` reads the flat layer map, which the
# boundary `scene_admit` opens through the interest service never enters, so it
# answers an invalid RID for every scene the scene plane itself admitted to.
#
# WHAT FORCED WHAT, swept rather than assumed. Nothing outside scene_core.gd
# named _observers, and the only writes to _pending_request_id were three cases
# in test_scene_interface.gd.
#
# ONE API CHANGE WAS FORCED and it is the reason those three cases changed
# rather than crossed. The record plane used to expose a settable pending id and
# a scenario could plant one; it no longer does, and it should not, because an
# id a caller can claim without opening lets it answer a request nobody made.
# open_request() is the only route now. The three cases draw an id instead of
# planting one, and so does the arm, and the rows the arm produces are the rows
# recorded BEFORE the port. Holding those fixed across a forced API change is
# what the golden is for.
#
# TWO INNER CLASSES ARE NOT TAKEN, and neither blocks this batch. _Admission is
# private and holds a Node and a NetwInterestLayer, so it cannot precede the
# interest port at W4. MoveOpts is PUBLIC, so ClassDB's flatness forces it to be
# separately registered or absorbed into the verb that takes it, and that is a
# decision on merits rather than a translation. TRIGGER: take MoveOpts when the
# move verb itself crosses; take _Admission with the interest layer at W4.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT is tests/crossing/scene_arm.gd against
# tests/native/goldens/scene_records.txt: 21 rows over 11 scenarios, recorded at
# d1bb6941 before a line of the record plane was native, and red-proven three
# times across three independent halves (a duplicate registration stored twice,
# a book that ignores its event key, a settled deadline that leaves its id
# behind). All 21 rows reproduced unchanged after the port.
#
# TWO HAZARDS THE PORT CREATED AND THE NATIVE CASES CLOSE, both absent from the
# GDScript original because it dispatched through a fresh Dictionary lookup per
# edge. A contiguous row moves when the table grows, so a callback that
# registers another observer during its own dispatch would leave the walk
# reading freed memory; and a pruning write-back that rolled the row back to
# what it started with would silently swallow whatever that callback added. N8
# and N9 state both, driven by a callable written for the purpose.
#
